  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Substituted ammonium salt is formed when chloroalkane reacted with excess of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, which on reaction with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>diaminobutane</mi></math>.</p><p>The carbon - halogen bond in alkyl halides can be easily cleaved by a nucleophile. Hence, an alkyl halide on reaction with an ethanolic solution of ammonia undergoes a nucleophilic substitution reaction in which the halogen atom is replaced by an amino group. This process of cleavage of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>–</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> bond by ammonia molecule is known as ammonolysis.  Substituted ammonium salt on reaction with sodium hydroxide gives 1,4-diaminobutane as shown below,</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/08d94b82-16e7-478f-92e5-17c71760cfb9.png\" style=\"width: 700px; height: 314px;\"/></p><p>Hence, the answer is option B.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
